ef 17592186045417] [17592186088647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "597d4650-d7ed-5642-9b5e-cf25ec638f05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088648 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088648 #edb/ref 1061 24234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d74a7f00-bd78-50fa-b35a-fe22c92c7804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088649 #edb/ref 1061 24235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088649 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59826183-4e40-52cf-85bf-49e587ced10a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088649 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088650 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088650 #edb/ref 1061 24236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088650 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31b88c4c-e4b0-5b75-8e76-d8013e805d0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088650 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088651 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088651 #edb/ref 1061 24237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22d3be62-0495-564f-8728-c69a96ff20e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088652 #edb/ref 1061 24238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87a6e8c8-6061-5698-9cbf-e13d800b6a02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088666 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088666 #edb/ref 1061 25327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088666 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e724fe89-8251-5f08-9463-a1bf1a894289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088666 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088667 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088667 #edb/ref 1061 25328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088667 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c889c748-9b68-50fe-a9f2-7282493522dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088667 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088825 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088825 #edb/ref 1061 607946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088825 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41c07f62-cd8a-521d-a6ef-1857ff038a27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088825 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088826 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088826 #edb/ref 1061 607953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088826 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21a255e2-1f22-52e2-a2f7-4dcfefdf1043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088826 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088863 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that our general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{(3 + 3 \\textrm{i})x} + Be^{(3 - 3 \\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nOur goal is to simplify this to find the **real-valued general solution**. We can do this using **Euler's formula**, which states that \n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos{\\theta} + \\textrm{i}\\sin{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we use the law of exponents to separate the real and imaginary parts in the exponential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x}{e}^{3\\text{i}x}+B{e}^{3x}{e}^{-3\\text{i}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(A{e}^{3\\text{i}x}+B{e}^{-3\\text{i}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply Euler's formula with $\\theta = 3x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{3x}(A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}((A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants, we can define new arbitrary constants $P=A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i}(A-B).$ The general solution becomes:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y = e^{3x}(P\\cos{3 x} + Q\\sin{3 x}).}\n$$\n\nThis is the final form of the solution, written entirely with real-valued functions.\n\nSo, for example,\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{3x}(5\\cos{3 x} - \\sin{3 x}),\n$$\n\ncorresponding to $P=5$ and $Q=-1,$ is a solution of the given differential equation. On the other hand,\n\n$$\ny(x) =  e^{3x}(\\cos{5 x} - \\sin{3 x})\n$$\n\nis *not* a solution because it contains a function $e^{3x}\\cos{5x},$ which is not included in the above form representing the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088863 #edb/ref 1065 14887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088863 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "25452a20-5d48-511d-b978-9a5f1c2b8894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088863 #edb/ref 1067 "Convert the Complex Solution to a Real-Valued Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088864 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall our solution from the previous slide:\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2\\textrm{i}x} + Be^{-2\\textrm{i}x}.\n$$\n\nWe use **Euler's formula**, which states \n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta,\n$$\n\n to rewrite the exponentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are typically solving for a real-world quantity, our final answer must be a real-valued function. For this to be true, the arbitrary constants $A$ and $B$ must be a complex conjugate pair ($B = \\overline{A}$). This implies that the following definitions yield *real* constants $P$ and $Q$:\n\n$$\nP = A+B \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q = \\textrm{i}(A-B).\n$$\n\nThis gives the **general real-valued solution**:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = P\\cos{2x} + Q\\sin{2x}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary real constants determined by initial conditions.\n\nSo, for example,\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\cos{2x} - 2\\sin{2x},\n$$\n\ncorresponding to $P=1$ and $Q=-2,$ is a solution of the given differential equation. On the other hand,\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\cos{2x} + \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis *not* a solution because it contains a function $\\sin{x},$ which is not included in the above form representing the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088864 #edb/ref 1065 14888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088864 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b126cbc0-4b1c-5a98-9c99-005a476f53c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088864 #edb/ref 1067 "Convert the Complex Solution to a Real-Valued Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088942 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **complex roots**. Let's see how to find the solution in this case.\n\nWe will solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y =0.\n$$\n\n**Step 1:** Find the characteristic equation.\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ we find the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the ODE gives the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0\\,(\\text{since }{e}^{\\lambda x}\\ne 0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for the characteristic roots.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots are a *complex conjugate pair*. Since the roots are distinct, the general solution is a linear combination of the corresponding exponentials:\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2\\textrm{i}x} + Be^{-2\\textrm{i}x}.\n$$\n\nOn the next slide, we will use *Euler's formula* to convert this into a more useful real-valued form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088942 #edb/ref 1065 4052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088942 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "18a414e1-5fae-5260-8980-5971fbd844e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088942 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088943 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE may have complex solutions. In such cases, the general solution will initially involve complex exponentials.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 18y =0.\n$$\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}$, we get the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 18 =0.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by *completing the square*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +18 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two complex roots are $\\lambda_1 = 3 + 3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2 = 3 - 3\\textrm{i}.$\n\nThe general solution is therefore\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{(3 + 3 \\textrm{i})x} + Be^{(3 - 3 \\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we will use *Euler's formula* to rewrite this solution in a form that does not involve complex numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088943 #edb/ref 1065 4053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088943 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d2be766f-b054-5431-aaa5-32c2129b9d92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088943 #edb/ref 1067 "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088957 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, if the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **imaginary roots** \n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\pm b\\textrm{i},\n$$\n\nthen the *general solution* is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{\\textrm{i} b x} + B e^{-\\textrm{i} bx}.\n$$\n\nUsing *Euler's formula*, the above can be simplified to\n\n$$\ny(x) = P \\cos bx + Q \\sin bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $P = A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i} (A-B).$\n\nLet's take a look at another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088957 #edb/ref 1065 4162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088957 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0cc2f848-e126-5ee8-84ea-809e08a92dff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088957 #edb/ref 1067 "A Shortcut When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088958 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, if the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has **complex conjugate roots** \n\n$$\n\\lambda = a \\pm b\\textrm{i},\n$$\n\n then the *general solution* is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{(a+b\\textrm{i})x} + B e^{(a-b\\textrm{i})x}.\n$$\n\nUsing *Euler's formula*, we can expand and group the terms:\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{ax} \\left[ (A+B)\\cos bx + \\textrm{i}(A-B)\\sin bx \\right].\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\ny(x) = e^{ax} \\left( P \\cos bx + Q \\sin bx \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P = A+B$ and $Q=\\textrm{i} (A-B).$\n\nLet's take a look at another example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088958 #edb/ref 1065 4163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088958 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "922b0814-7e51-5155-9464-ccf52671087e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088958 #edb/ref 1067 "A Shortcut When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264079 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264079 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264079 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7d4696d5-cc1e-5203-b64d-8bf1bd1f0021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264395 #edb/ref 1080 "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264395 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264395 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a4016bef-beb0-5db0-82a4-44e41b9e2b9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264682 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264682 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264682 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c1a592ed-3d1c-533a-acce-12ce3e8a08bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264880 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264880 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264880 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d7acfab0-f973-57d7-848f-7c92141ba2a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265236 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265236 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265236 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe0aed58-ec3b-559f-a069-761233a8e9d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186264682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265335 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "324549a9-80bb-5ccf-ab12-74bb3177f177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265886 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265886 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265886 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265886 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91a61312-3bc9-5174-b905-87935cc4c9cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265886 #edb/ref 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$ are the purely imaginary roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345815 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345815 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345815 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "35f38504-8305-5326-bb00-99730398c72f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346566 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346566 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346566 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c2c2f19-a310-51bc-b9f3-c3da0cbade4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346996 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346996 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346996 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bd394e25-e934-5d93-8427-bbaa8ee6cec9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347447 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{x}